WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.500
Conflict, the Red Wolves are on the prowl.

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:26.760
And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:28.700
I'm Mike. And I'm Sean.

00:00:28.700 --> 00:00:30.080
And that's it.

00:00:30.600 --> 00:00:37.400
And today we are talking war games, specifically a conflict of sorts.

00:00:37.740 --> 00:00:39.120
With Matthew Broderick?

00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:41.100
No, no.

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:43.680
In fact, actually, yeah, you bring up a good point.

00:00:43.840 --> 00:00:46.500
Like war games might have been a better title here.

00:00:46.920 --> 00:00:49.100
Conflict is just one word, right?

00:00:49.820 --> 00:00:53.780
I think Joe said conflict could just be what happens in any game.

00:00:53.880 --> 00:00:55.580
So it's not quite descriptive.

00:00:55.880 --> 00:00:57.480
But it is a conflict, right?

00:00:57.480 --> 00:01:01.880
It's a fight of two armies, basically going to war.

00:01:02.020 --> 00:01:03.460
So why didn't we call this war again?

00:01:03.560 --> 00:01:05.320
War is better than conflict, actually.

00:01:05.440 --> 00:01:08.120
I can think of a thousand better titles in one minute than conflict.

00:01:08.120 --> 00:01:23.300
It seems like a title you'd give something that has so much gravitas that you can only name it the most primal or the most base descriptor.

00:01:23.300 --> 00:01:25.380
Like that's the only thing that fits.

00:01:26.100 --> 00:01:29.680
But it really like, no, you got to give it some flavor.

00:01:29.680 --> 00:01:37.000
This is already going to play into my criticisms in the future.

00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:38.340
Yeah, no, you're right.

00:01:38.460 --> 00:01:43.800
Like when you when you see the title of the game, you make an opinion based off of that.

00:01:43.800 --> 00:01:47.640
I didn't know who Lolo was in the adventures of Lolo.

00:01:47.820 --> 00:01:51.880
But it seemed like it had a whimsical fun to it.

00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:53.900
You know, oh, the adventures of Lolo.

00:01:54.060 --> 00:01:57.140
Right. Like I wasn't expecting to play Rambo first blood.

00:01:57.320 --> 00:01:59.880
Right. You know, it's there's a different tone there.

00:01:59.880 --> 00:02:08.960
And yet with conflict, I don't know what kind of game I'm going to be playing, but it is it's you know, you're doing, you know, combat strategy of war.

00:02:09.140 --> 00:02:11.240
And usually there's two different kinds of games for that.

00:02:11.320 --> 00:02:24.960
There's the turn based war fight where, you know, you you take a turn, choose your commands, and then the opponent chooses their commands and you fight based on where you are in the map and stuff like that.

00:02:24.960 --> 00:02:30.620
And that even applies to like the board game side of things like that's how risk works risk is a turn based war game.

00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:41.860
And then and then you have the real time strategy games where you're constantly sending out and controlling your troops from this like general point of view.

00:02:42.060 --> 00:02:48.900
And then you're building new stuff and you're sending out larger armies as you're preparing to for the fight and everything.

00:02:49.040 --> 00:02:50.600
But it's all happening in real time.

00:02:50.840 --> 00:02:53.220
Where do you stand on that before we get into conflict?

00:02:53.220 --> 00:02:55.840
Like, like, like, are you a turn based guy or a real time guy?

00:02:56.660 --> 00:03:12.360
No, I am a turn based guy because I I never developed the sort of twitch reflexes that real time kind of requires because in a real time game, you're trying to control everything at once.

00:03:12.360 --> 00:03:13.480
And that goes without saying.

00:03:13.920 --> 00:03:17.380
But there's like actual like that's why it's an e-sport like it.

00:03:17.480 --> 00:03:27.840
You can you can kind of the actions that you're taking to control it still requires twitch reflexes, even though it's a strategy game.

00:03:28.180 --> 00:03:33.100
And I prefer strategy games where strategy is sort of where it starts and ends.

00:03:33.100 --> 00:03:35.080
I like to think about what I'm going to do.

00:03:35.280 --> 00:03:43.340
Yeah. And turn based also get, you know, presents the information to you in a way that is digestible before you make the move.

00:03:43.340 --> 00:03:48.200
Whereas in real time, you're you're seeing it all happen.

00:03:48.480 --> 00:03:49.860
And yes, there is information.

00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:57.340
You know, sometimes it's as comic booky as numbers popping up on screen of the damage you're, you know, units are doing to the other units.

00:03:57.340 --> 00:04:09.220
But then there's also the surprise factor of, you know, if if you're caught off guard by your positioning while you're doing something else and then you didn't realize that this was happening on the other end of the battlefield.

00:04:10.040 --> 00:04:12.240
You know, there's a lot there's a lot of different things going on there.

00:04:12.240 --> 00:04:24.300
And while I would say conflict falls in, you know, to the turn based side with the hexagon style map and the, you know, your turn first, then the opponent's turn.

00:04:24.500 --> 00:04:37.640
There are some interesting like things that I usually see reserved just for the real time strategy genre, such as having these factories where you continuously make units while the war goes on.

00:04:37.760 --> 00:04:39.400
Usually that's a real time thing.

00:04:39.560 --> 00:04:40.640
And that was surprising to see here.

00:04:41.260 --> 00:04:48.260
Yeah, so it's it's kind of got a weird combination of real time and turn based aspects.

00:04:48.260 --> 00:04:56.580
I don't really know if that stuff's been codified yet because I don't think there's been a real time strategy game released at all yet.

00:04:57.320 --> 00:05:05.500
I could be wrong, but I just don't think that even computers are really at the level that that can be done.

00:05:05.500 --> 00:05:12.660
So I feel like every war game is turn based in the computer and console market at this point.

00:05:14.120 --> 00:05:26.060
But yeah, usually in a turn based game, I guess like civilization is the and that's not even really the same thing as civilization is more than just a war game.

00:05:26.060 --> 00:05:28.880
But like, yeah, you have factories, but that's just your cities.

00:05:29.020 --> 00:05:40.240
So I guess there's some stuff that that's borrowed and and and played with in the future that like this game and I'm sure some others have sort of put the groundwork for.

00:05:40.240 --> 00:05:51.880
But it's really interesting to see like the kind of when when there were really rules for this stuff, what they're actually working with.

00:05:51.880 --> 00:06:03.300
Yeah, just to go back on the were there real time strategy games, I believe on one of our Nostalgia Bites episode, Boko Suko Wars was described as that kind of game.

00:06:03.460 --> 00:06:09.020
And we were firmly in the camp of it's not and it's not even it's barely any strategy to begin.

00:06:09.240 --> 00:06:10.680
Yeah, that is not what I mean.

00:06:10.940 --> 00:06:17.360
Right, right, right. That's not the kind of game that's if that's what they're considering in that same genre, then that genre has a long way to grow.

00:06:17.820 --> 00:06:20.180
That's like a Pikmin game basically anyway.

00:06:20.180 --> 00:06:33.860
So and so with with conflict here, you also mentioned a civilization which has a lot more in common than just the hexagon style grid, which actually civilization wouldn't really adopt until five.

00:06:34.520 --> 00:06:36.060
Yeah. So not until five.

00:06:36.260 --> 00:06:40.600
But there are some elements, too, in these war games that are I feel like are necessary.

00:06:40.600 --> 00:06:43.680
They're not they're not copying civilization by any means.

00:06:43.800 --> 00:06:45.600
But, you know, there's towns, too.

00:06:45.600 --> 00:06:47.360
There's types of terrains.

00:06:47.500 --> 00:06:50.480
And those things also matter in the scheme of war.

00:06:50.480 --> 00:07:01.840
And in games like Nobunaga's Ambition and the other Koei games that have that format where you are doing a little bit of civilization where you're taking care of your your civilization.

00:07:01.840 --> 00:07:03.680
But you're also then going to war.

00:07:03.900 --> 00:07:07.380
The clunky part in that game is always when you go to war.

00:07:07.660 --> 00:07:11.860
Right. Then it has like this war map that is, you know, it has multiple terrains.

00:07:11.860 --> 00:07:16.400
And you've got to figure out, like, how do I move my units and am I prepared for these fights?

00:07:16.720 --> 00:07:27.040
So I'm kind of glad that conflict chose to be like, yeah, I mean, like there's towns and there's forests, but they're just terrain things like you don't have to manage them by any means.

00:07:27.160 --> 00:07:29.240
You're you're just managing your units.

00:07:29.240 --> 00:07:32.980
And that's because really they're presenting you with an arena.

00:07:33.260 --> 00:07:44.140
And like you said, the towns and the airstrips, that's all just sort of that's just there, not just for set dressing, but that's just playing with terrain.

00:07:44.940 --> 00:07:51.920
So it's a very different kind of game than Nobunaga's Ambition and Romance to the Three Kingdoms.

00:07:52.020 --> 00:08:00.100
But when you bring those up, that's like it definitely falls into the same category for me.

00:08:00.340 --> 00:08:06.920
I know they play very differently, but they are still these war game aspects.

00:08:06.920 --> 00:08:19.540
And I'll wait until we get more into the nitty gritty to for like my my pure view, but definitely of the same ilk as those games.

00:08:19.760 --> 00:08:27.560
And it's funny because the towns are so much just a, you know, a part of the land that that's not the goal.

00:08:27.720 --> 00:08:32.240
Like your goal in these arenas isn't to destroy the cities or take them over.

00:08:32.420 --> 00:08:35.080
It's to destroy the commanding unit.

00:08:35.080 --> 00:08:37.560
I believe it's a flag tank.

00:08:37.920 --> 00:08:41.740
I don't know if that's an actual like type of tank or if that's just how they refer to them.

00:08:41.960 --> 00:08:44.020
Yeah, I think it's like the flagship, but it's a tank.

00:08:44.260 --> 00:08:46.440
Yeah. But yeah, that's your goal every time.

00:08:46.580 --> 00:08:52.400
It's not even brute force like it is in some games of civilization that you try to take over.

00:08:52.500 --> 00:08:56.600
Like you basically just have to total domination over your opponents.

00:08:56.660 --> 00:09:03.940
In this one, as long as you can get to the flag tank and get around everything else, you don't have to destroy the remaining units.

00:09:03.940 --> 00:09:05.420
It's just that's the mercy call.

00:09:05.600 --> 00:09:16.900
So there are 16 scenarios in this game and each one is just a different arena with a different terrain and a different starting position.

00:09:17.120 --> 00:09:19.000
But the units don't change.

00:09:19.180 --> 00:09:21.440
The units aren't locked off or anything like that.

00:09:21.440 --> 00:09:26.100
And the opponents are just again, it's just the scenario has changed.

00:09:26.340 --> 00:09:28.780
So for one, it's an all land battle.

00:09:28.900 --> 00:09:31.340
Another one that you're separated into two islands.

00:09:31.560 --> 00:09:41.140
Another one, it's like an interesting like stop gap where there's only a small narrow passageway between the two opposing teams.

00:09:41.140 --> 00:09:43.680
So how are you going to strategically fight around that?

00:09:43.880 --> 00:09:45.680
I like all those ideas.

00:09:46.280 --> 00:09:56.440
But it is a little weird that at the end of the day, the 16 scenarios are really just 16 different ways to play what is essentially the same mission over and over again.

00:09:56.600 --> 00:09:59.100
Destroying the AI computers flagship.

00:09:59.880 --> 00:10:09.580
So yeah, the one thing, this is sort of an aside, I think what would have if you really like the core gameplay of this game.

00:10:10.500 --> 00:10:13.420
And I think that there is something here.

00:10:13.600 --> 00:10:20.480
But if you really like the core gameplay of this game, this is a game ripe for having like a map creator.

00:10:20.660 --> 00:10:32.080
Like we've seen it in so many different games that don't really call for one that it almost feels like it's wrong that this one doesn't have it.

00:10:33.380 --> 00:10:47.560
But yeah, like basically all the maps are just different ways to explore the the core gameplay of these sort of like rock, paper, scissors thing that's happening every time you get into a battle.

00:10:47.860 --> 00:10:57.960
And each of the different hexes, the types of hexes, at least having like offensive and defensive modifiers.

00:10:58.560 --> 00:11:03.680
And I think one of the things that I find very confusing about this game is that's very opaque.

00:11:03.960 --> 00:11:06.260
I don't know. I know what the modifiers should be.

00:11:06.360 --> 00:11:08.520
I don't know how they're affecting the actual game.

00:11:08.800 --> 00:11:10.380
I'm rambling right now.

00:11:10.740 --> 00:11:17.620
But yeah, no, I think it's a it's a great point because there is there in the manual itself.

00:11:17.860 --> 00:11:25.480
Right. It tries its best to and I would say that it does a good job of explaining what the expectations of how you should play the game are,

00:11:25.480 --> 00:11:33.340
because that's an issue that we run into a lot in these larger, you know, strategy games is, well, what you know, what am I what do I need to focus on?

00:11:33.460 --> 00:11:41.100
And so they do break it down from just a movement production, then battle how you supply like I get all that stuff.

00:11:41.540 --> 00:11:50.680
But other than a little like relations guide at the end that shows you like your your unit and then the opposing unit.

00:11:50.680 --> 00:11:57.380
And it's a very complicated grid that, you know, I with different symbols and X's and circles.

00:11:57.740 --> 00:12:03.460
Yeah. They have a key and it translates to like, oh, well, this unit versus that unit means total victory.

00:12:03.460 --> 00:12:06.880
And this universe of that unit is you're at an advantage.

00:12:06.880 --> 00:12:10.220
Right. But but still, that's only true.

00:12:10.220 --> 00:12:25.440
If you actually know what you're doing in the combat, because this isn't a combat where you just, you know, go you're in the hexagon next to the weaker unit and you just say, I'd like to fire at that unit.

00:12:25.580 --> 00:12:28.140
Like that's not how combat works in this game.

00:12:28.280 --> 00:12:34.780
Combat is a lot more involved if you want it to be, which is an interesting thing, you know.

00:12:34.780 --> 00:12:44.940
So that's something that I did not do was have the computer take care of all of combat because I felt like I needed to know what that part of the game was.

00:12:45.080 --> 00:12:50.620
But that is an option to just be the general that like pushes the pieces together.

00:12:50.840 --> 00:12:53.360
And that's a very unsatisfying thing, actually.

00:12:53.520 --> 00:12:54.800
Like they should have they should have.

00:12:54.900 --> 00:12:58.460
If anything, it should be normal combat mode and simple combat mode.

00:12:58.460 --> 00:13:12.040
You should still have to be the one who decides the actions of your units, because if you're just placing them on the map, I would argue that you're doing the weakest part of a strategy game.

00:13:12.180 --> 00:13:16.300
You know, like you're just thinking about which unit should attack the other unit.

00:13:16.300 --> 00:13:28.580
And I'd say that the actual battle part of deciding how they go about successfully taking down or dodging the the enemy unit is the core like interesting part of this game.

00:13:29.220 --> 00:13:38.160
Yeah. And I mean, like if you're looking at war in Civ, that's like the the easy mode or the simple mode.

00:13:38.320 --> 00:13:41.020
That's sort of how war works in that game.

00:13:41.020 --> 00:13:46.920
And I wouldn't say that that's a dumb game, but I guess it's also war is also just a part of that game.

00:13:47.540 --> 00:13:48.340
It doesn't.

00:13:48.980 --> 00:13:53.460
And it also has like economic and cultural and whatever else victories.

00:13:54.140 --> 00:13:59.240
So I guess it's OK that it the war gaming itself is a little simplified.

00:13:59.240 --> 00:14:15.880
But I think that there is still enough strategy in placing pieces and like knowing advantages and oh, should I attack from this position because it's from a forest or should I instead attack from this position because it has higher ground?

00:14:15.880 --> 00:14:31.020
Like I don't I think that the simple mode could have still worked, but I needed to play the more complex mode where you decide to fight and then you go into like a battle screen.

00:14:31.560 --> 00:14:35.760
Now, the battle screen is very like it's simple.

00:14:35.980 --> 00:14:42.200
There's three things you could choose to do and then six sub things you could do from there.

00:14:42.200 --> 00:14:50.860
But the problem here is you're not getting a lot of feedback as to what any of that really means.

00:14:51.760 --> 00:14:55.860
And the feedback that you do get can be misleading.

00:14:56.140 --> 00:14:59.040
So let me just give another little example here.

00:14:59.240 --> 00:15:04.000
You can attack, defend or try to escape.

00:15:04.520 --> 00:15:10.240
If you have let's say this is the first turn and you have decided to battle.

00:15:10.240 --> 00:15:22.280
Now, if you attack, you're going to attack and then the opponent can choose to do some kind of evasive maneuver so that they can get into a better position to counterattack.

00:15:22.580 --> 00:15:28.260
But then they can also try to escape and they will.

00:15:28.460 --> 00:15:34.480
The game will say that this tank has done this maneuver and escaped.

00:15:34.660 --> 00:15:39.560
And then the next screen will say that it got bombed out and exploded.

00:15:40.640 --> 00:15:44.380
And it's not quite clear what happened between those two.

00:15:45.420 --> 00:15:50.700
Exactly. Like there's not a lot of, I mean, I guess trial and error.

00:15:51.300 --> 00:16:00.220
And that's sort of like the theme of these strategy games from the day that we've been playing over this podcast.

00:16:00.380 --> 00:16:02.080
It's all just trial and error.

00:16:02.080 --> 00:16:09.220
You're never going to get very clear feedback from the game itself of like saying like, maybe you should try this.

00:16:09.460 --> 00:16:11.860
It's just you have to brute force it.

00:16:12.020 --> 00:16:14.720
And that's what every battle is.

00:16:14.720 --> 00:16:16.140
It's very drawn out.

00:16:16.340 --> 00:16:19.940
You're going to fail even if you have the advantaged unit.

00:16:20.820 --> 00:16:22.480
It's kind of screwy.

00:16:22.480 --> 00:16:27.660
And it's a shame because the combat dialogue presented by the game.

00:16:27.820 --> 00:16:36.400
Keep in mind, all this action stuff is happening in a very small screen where it just shows the unit that you are and the opposing unit when it's there.

00:16:36.580 --> 00:16:37.780
When it's their opposing turns.

00:16:37.780 --> 00:16:45.740
But it shows you like in a UI, your strength, your weapon of choice, and then the enemy's strength.

00:16:45.740 --> 00:16:50.180
And if they have a weapon or not, what their weapon ammunition is.

00:16:50.300 --> 00:16:52.160
And then there's the command choices and stuff.

00:16:52.260 --> 00:16:57.540
And obviously for if you're playing as blue, which you have to in a one player game, you have your choices.

00:16:57.540 --> 00:16:59.260
And then red automatically picks theirs.

00:16:59.260 --> 00:17:05.520
But there's this little box, a dialogue box in the middle that feels like it's giving a lot of flavor.

00:17:05.620 --> 00:17:10.240
But in an unsatisfying way, again, because I've only said like attack.

00:17:10.580 --> 00:17:13.400
And then it's added all this extra detail in, which is fun.

00:17:13.520 --> 00:17:18.380
But I can't tell how much of it actually like meant something or was a dice roll.

00:17:18.720 --> 00:17:22.460
So it's not actually giving you any information.

00:17:22.460 --> 00:17:29.320
So it'll always say if you are in a jet, like you're attacking in a jet or you're fighting a jet.

00:17:29.900 --> 00:17:34.480
And it has done a specific, let's say it's dove.

00:17:34.880 --> 00:17:37.580
Like that's one of the evasive maneuvers it can be.

00:17:38.520 --> 00:17:44.000
It'll always just say that the G-force really affected the pilot.

00:17:44.700 --> 00:17:51.100
And it has nothing to do with like if the dive is successful, if that is like actually evaded the shot.

00:17:51.360 --> 00:17:54.420
Because the next screen will be a hit or a miss.

00:17:54.620 --> 00:17:58.780
But the previous screen will always just say the same thing.

00:17:58.780 --> 00:18:00.860
So it's not really informing you of anything.

00:18:00.940 --> 00:18:02.640
It is just flavor.

00:18:02.900 --> 00:18:06.040
And it's tough to say if that's good or bad.

00:18:06.040 --> 00:18:15.620
I think it's bad because we're both in the camp of like give me as much information as possible to know what I'm doing strategically.

00:18:15.940 --> 00:18:19.300
Like I need to know like it's almost like the Fire Emblem way of things, right?

00:18:19.340 --> 00:18:29.420
You want to know what actual damage you're going to be doing to the enemy unit before you commit to the action, right?

00:18:29.420 --> 00:18:36.140
And even though like Fire Emblem will even be like there's an 80% chance of this happening, you're willing to take that kind of risk.

00:18:36.300 --> 00:18:44.100
But here it's more like I'm going to say attack and I'm supposed to have the advantage, but God knows how much damage it's going to do.

00:18:44.320 --> 00:18:46.240
Yeah, if it does any damage.

00:18:46.320 --> 00:18:47.380
Right, if it does any damage.

00:18:47.760 --> 00:18:57.080
And the defense of the computers feels like, you know, maybe they, I mean, obviously they have a better understanding of their defensive maneuvers than me.

00:18:57.080 --> 00:19:05.040
But I just felt like it says that I have total victory over these guys or that I'm at least at an advantage.

00:19:05.040 --> 00:19:09.180
And I feel like they know something I don't about the defensive maneuvers.

00:19:09.300 --> 00:19:11.440
Like they know how to choose the right one or something.

00:19:11.760 --> 00:19:16.480
Yeah. Again, this will be the last little rant that I go on here.

00:19:16.640 --> 00:19:24.440
But there also seems to be something going on, some calculation, some system that tells you.

00:19:24.440 --> 00:19:30.780
So, okay, in a battle, you'll have, yeah, the attack, the defend, and the retreat.

00:19:32.380 --> 00:19:35.860
And you don't always have all three of those options.

00:19:36.200 --> 00:19:39.480
You can end up only being able to defend and retreat.

00:19:40.260 --> 00:19:44.300
And retreat will give you the same like sub options of defend.

00:19:44.300 --> 00:19:49.560
And it seems to be completely random if you're able to counterattack.

00:19:49.820 --> 00:19:59.740
Like the, I will initiate combat as a surface to air tank against a helicopter.

00:19:59.960 --> 00:20:02.980
And I will shoot my weapon and it'll miss.

00:20:03.120 --> 00:20:04.200
And then they'll do something.

00:20:04.440 --> 00:20:05.820
And then they get to counterattack.

00:20:05.820 --> 00:20:08.400
And then it'll come back to me.

00:20:08.700 --> 00:20:10.820
And I can only defend a retreat.

00:20:11.120 --> 00:20:13.120
And I don't know what's going on there.

00:20:13.260 --> 00:20:20.060
I don't know if it's like, oh, I picked the, I should have, um, I should have turned instead of adjusted.

00:20:20.380 --> 00:20:22.200
And I don't know what the difference is there.

00:20:22.280 --> 00:20:28.220
But I think that has some effect on whether I can counterattack or not.

00:20:28.400 --> 00:20:35.480
You know what I mean? Like, there's so much behind the curtain that it just doesn't seem to make any sense.

00:20:35.700 --> 00:20:44.080
Right. And I feel like that's why the actual way to like learn this game is to do the auto battle mode.

00:20:44.220 --> 00:20:46.540
The one that we kind of hinted at a second ago.

00:20:46.540 --> 00:20:57.380
So, uh, it basically, if you, um, press select, you'll be able to, uh, get this screen that determines the, uh, the scroll speed of the messages.

00:20:57.960 --> 00:21:04.660
And, uh, the, uh, whether you or the computer is in charge of the combat.

00:21:04.880 --> 00:21:10.380
And when you choose the computer, it's not like it just happens and you don't get to see what happens.

00:21:10.460 --> 00:21:14.460
You actually get to watch the computer fight the other computer.

00:21:14.600 --> 00:21:16.440
And there's something to be learned there.

00:21:16.540 --> 00:21:20.140
Especially with the amount of units that we have in this game.

00:21:20.380 --> 00:21:31.860
Uh, the blue and red do have, even though it seems like mostly the same units, they do have different, uh, movement capabilities and weapons and, um, you know, and, and strength and stuff like that.

00:21:32.120 --> 00:21:35.920
So there's a lot of like variables in this.

00:21:35.920 --> 00:21:44.520
And if you really want to complete all 15 scenarios, you do have to get a good control over the various units because there's land units.

00:21:44.660 --> 00:21:48.200
There's, uh, there's like cargo units and supply trucks.

00:21:48.380 --> 00:21:53.280
There's different air units, uh, that aren't just like, you know, it's not just fighter jets.

00:21:53.280 --> 00:21:54.660
Like there's other different types here.

00:21:54.880 --> 00:22:01.140
So to learn all of that in a game where the manual doesn't necessarily spell out for you what you should be.

00:22:01.240 --> 00:22:08.660
I mean, it has a defensive commands thing that is arbitrarily decided by like maneuver one, maneuver two, all the way up to maneuver six.

00:22:08.660 --> 00:22:14.020
And then it says like maneuver one, because it doesn't want to give it a name for your particular unit.

00:22:14.320 --> 00:22:20.660
The chances of avoiding enemy fire are not, are not so high, but this can set up a good counter attack.

00:22:20.680 --> 00:22:23.240
And it's like, I don't know if that's enough to go.

00:22:23.340 --> 00:22:25.340
I don't know. Yeah. I don't know if that's enough to go off of.

00:22:25.420 --> 00:22:27.560
I want to see what the computer would do in that situation.

00:22:27.560 --> 00:22:31.600
And so, uh, there is something, a bit of a learning curve here.

00:22:31.920 --> 00:22:37.860
And the best way to do that is to play on auto actually, and let the computer kind of show you the ropes.

00:22:38.040 --> 00:22:39.820
That sounds so boring.

00:22:45.120 --> 00:22:49.360
Okay. Let's talk about something that also would affect whether this game is boring or not.

00:22:49.480 --> 00:22:52.080
What's the pace of play like in this game?

00:22:52.080 --> 00:23:01.840
At the beginning of the game, it lets you decide whether each, you and the computer are going to control three units on the map between turns or all of the units.

00:23:02.020 --> 00:23:11.600
And, uh, this basically just, you know, will change the nature of the game too, because it's, it's more than just saying like, all right, let's speed this up and just only get control of three.

00:23:11.760 --> 00:23:20.360
It's actually changing, you know, strategy of like who you're bringing forward in the battlefield, because it's not like you're going to move three guys forward.

00:23:20.360 --> 00:23:25.040
And then the next turn say, you know what, actually, I think I'm going to bring these three guys forward as well.

00:23:25.160 --> 00:23:27.260
Like you're losing time in that regard.

00:23:28.000 --> 00:23:33.860
So, yeah, that's an interesting decision that they made to have these be options.

00:23:34.040 --> 00:23:42.680
Yeah. Either move three at a time, both the computer and the player or player one and player two, or move all at once.

00:23:42.680 --> 00:24:00.340
Um, now I know for a fact that the reason that they made this an option is because if they didn't make three, uh, movements, the default, all games would be plotting messes that people would get completely.

00:24:00.340 --> 00:24:08.080
Just, just, they would, their eyes would glaze over because the game would be slower than anything we've played before.

00:24:08.760 --> 00:24:14.240
Um, but that also, like you said, raises, it also adds wrinkles to the strategy.

00:24:14.240 --> 00:24:15.300
And I really liked that.

00:24:15.500 --> 00:24:19.260
Like, oh, do I move these three or do I move these three units?

00:24:19.260 --> 00:24:39.720
Um, do I try and like push forward and, uh, like man the, the assault or do I move these units into like occupying an airfield and get some freedom points or whatever you call them and try and build this up?

00:24:39.720 --> 00:24:47.520
And then I gotta make sure that I, I build my unit during these three turns so it doesn't just automatically go to the opponent.

00:24:47.880 --> 00:24:52.280
And so there, it adds some complexity to it.

00:24:52.560 --> 00:24:57.040
Um, but yeah, I think it was definitely a decision just based on pacing.

00:24:57.240 --> 00:24:59.060
It's a long game ahead of you.

00:24:59.200 --> 00:25:07.000
This is not like scenario one is just, um, you know, it's easy in scale and that's, that's a place you could spend some time.

00:25:07.000 --> 00:25:09.900
Like even the first scenario is going to be a long one.

00:25:09.900 --> 00:25:18.900
When you consider that on top of being able to control all units, if you wish, you're making new units to also continue the fight.

00:25:19.100 --> 00:25:29.900
So for the most part, you're playing, um, a strictly defensive game because you want to make sure that your flags, uh, your flag tank is not exposed.

00:25:29.900 --> 00:25:39.900
And then you're kind of just pushing forward gingerly with your, uh, you know, with your land units and taking your air units as more of a, uh, wide surround.

00:25:39.900 --> 00:25:50.760
So you do kind of wind up, especially because the air units aren't locked to these interesting land terrains because they can explore as they feel like you are mostly playing the same kind of game.

00:25:50.900 --> 00:26:05.440
But it is a, uh, I guess the best solution, unless you know the maps inside and out, the best solution to, to, to approach these is always the same style of protect your flag tank and, uh, and approach cautious.

00:26:06.160 --> 00:26:17.280
Yeah. And yeah, that's, it's funny that that's sort of the, the strategy that by default is kind of the most, uh, reliable.

00:26:17.540 --> 00:26:28.780
It's sort of the equivalent of an XCOM, just sort of moving up a little bit and then going into Overwatch and just, just dragging the game along that way.

00:26:28.780 --> 00:26:39.440
So much so that in the sequel, they have to change the mechanics so that doing that is actually counterintuitive because it's the optimal way to play, obviously.

00:26:39.900 --> 00:26:44.860
Um, so it's funny that the game is by design, so conservative.

00:26:45.420 --> 00:26:54.940
Um, and I learned that the hard way because I tried to just sort of barrel in guns blazing and my entire like forward operation was wiped out.

00:26:54.940 --> 00:27:01.340
And I mean, obviously it takes a while for that to happen, but yeah, I learned that.

00:27:02.100 --> 00:27:09.700
Modern turn-based games have that problem too, with showing you how far the enemy can extend itself, right?

00:27:09.780 --> 00:27:13.220
Like what their range of attack is based on where they currently are.

00:27:13.220 --> 00:27:16.440
And so that you kind of leave your units on the outskirt, right?

00:27:16.620 --> 00:27:23.820
Get everybody, uh, reformatted and aligned if they were previously broken up because of a battle that you took care of.

00:27:23.920 --> 00:27:30.200
Uh, again, you know, I'm working off a Fire Emblem because I just recently played, uh, Fire Emblem Engage.

00:27:30.340 --> 00:27:34.560
But that's a real problem because the enemy, you know that they can't reach you.

00:27:34.740 --> 00:27:36.740
You're able to see how far they can extend.

00:27:36.740 --> 00:27:42.960
And so as long as you don't come into their zone of conflict, they won't approach you.

00:27:43.140 --> 00:27:48.800
And so you can just take your sweet time on the battlefield, like resetting up.

00:27:48.940 --> 00:27:52.140
And I feel like that's something that should definitely be solved then.

00:27:52.280 --> 00:27:57.500
But even here, like there is a little bit of, imagine you're playing this game, Sean,

00:27:57.800 --> 00:28:00.720
against somebody who owned it since 1990.

00:28:01.100 --> 00:28:03.760
Okay. Like you're not playing, you know, you're not talking with me anymore.

00:28:03.760 --> 00:28:08.520
You're talking about somebody who's loved and played conflict for over 30 years.

00:28:08.740 --> 00:28:14.460
And you are screwed because, because they really know these systems inside and out.

00:28:14.620 --> 00:28:18.520
They know exactly how many movement units, uh, each can make.

00:28:18.740 --> 00:28:23.440
And, you know, there's the, who, who beats who and which defensive maneuvers to use.

00:28:23.520 --> 00:28:29.540
This is a game where actually the computer might be playing a little bit of softball with you compared to a pro player.

00:28:31.000 --> 00:28:32.080
I mean, yeah.

00:28:32.080 --> 00:28:38.640
I mean, I don't, it didn't seem like the computer was making very calculated decisions.

00:28:39.000 --> 00:28:46.320
Like, um, there's probably some skeleton of a strategy baked into the AI.

00:28:46.320 --> 00:28:54.240
Um, but there were definitely some boneheaded moves that even as a rookie, I could see like, you sure about that?

00:28:54.700 --> 00:29:05.640
Um, but it obviously, because it knows the, like the, the more granular details of how battles work, it's definitely an advantage there.

00:29:05.640 --> 00:29:13.180
But yeah, at the, at the, um, the general level, I don't really know if the computer knows what it's doing.

00:29:13.960 --> 00:29:17.260
Sean, you called these freedom points, which I really like.

00:29:17.360 --> 00:29:21.540
I appreciate you saying freedom points because, uh, they're fame points.

00:29:21.720 --> 00:29:27.520
Fame, yes. And, uh, you get them by occupying cities, airports, destroying units.

00:29:27.760 --> 00:29:29.200
So pretty much the opposite of freedom.

00:29:29.660 --> 00:29:31.180
But, uh, it's freedom.

00:29:31.400 --> 00:29:35.460
It's, you know, you're just expanding your country's freedom onto their country.

00:29:35.900 --> 00:29:40.680
This is like the, the currency to get higher tier units, right?

00:29:40.680 --> 00:29:45.240
Yes. Yeah. The more fame points the player has, the more powerful units they can produce.

00:29:45.540 --> 00:29:50.820
So, uh, you know, at the start of the game, if you have a certain amount of fame points, you can only have like infantry.

00:29:50.840 --> 00:29:53.480
And then when you finally get enough, now you can have tanks.

00:29:53.600 --> 00:29:59.680
And what's funny about that is that the computer always starts with more fame points than the player.

00:29:59.880 --> 00:30:00.960
Double. Yes.

00:30:01.140 --> 00:30:06.840
And the, the reason for that is probably because there isn't much of a challenge.

00:30:06.840 --> 00:30:11.840
If you're available to the same, uh, production of units.

00:30:12.260 --> 00:30:15.320
Do you think, do you think it was a mistake to double or no?

00:30:16.180 --> 00:30:36.760
I mean, I guess the, the actual difference is that like that can be debated, but even today, uh, strategy game AI is such a problem that like in Stellaris or in any grand strategy game or even in civilization, like a lot of the ways that,

00:30:36.840 --> 00:30:40.880
difficulty is adjusted is just by letting the computer cheat more.

00:30:41.540 --> 00:30:54.180
And when you get to hard, it just means that it can do 50% or a hundred percent more production at a particular unit than you can, because it's not as smart as you.

00:30:54.320 --> 00:31:02.040
So it's, it's funny to see it just like worn as a badge in this game, like to be so transparent about it.

00:31:02.080 --> 00:31:05.060
Uh, cause usually that sort of information is just hidden from you.

00:31:05.400 --> 00:31:06.420
Modern games.

00:31:07.420 --> 00:31:17.880
I have to think that the fame points are the only way in Victor Kai, the developer of the game who wasn't just one person, no matter how many times we joke about it.

00:31:18.260 --> 00:31:21.600
Um, that was their way of solving that.

00:31:21.760 --> 00:31:34.720
You wouldn't just camp forever because if you, if you do camp, you are basically waiting for an onslaught of really strong units while your fame stays the same or depletes as their stronger units make their way over to you.

00:31:34.720 --> 00:31:58.260
So it's probably a bit of a balancing thing just to encourage you to get over to the opponent faster, take out their units to get your own fame points and then grow stronger units so that you can continuously chip away at their, uh, you know, eventually their towns and their airports, which are not, you know, they're not as easy to, to get ahold of as the, as the units.

00:31:58.260 --> 00:32:07.760
Absolutely. All that to say, you know, forget about the computer for a second, forget about the fame points in, in the sense of like them having more than you.

00:32:07.760 --> 00:32:16.720
What do you think of the, the balance of this is, you know, blue versus red instead of the iconic halo series, red versus blue.

00:32:16.940 --> 00:32:20.720
Uh, what do you think of the balance between the two?

00:32:21.060 --> 00:32:23.760
Uh, I did mention that there were different types of units.

00:32:23.900 --> 00:32:32.600
You don't have to get into the nitty gritty of it or whatever, but just from a, uh, from a surface level understanding of how the game works with the time that we had with it.

00:32:32.600 --> 00:32:41.180
Uh, uh, did they, uh, did they create a game that is compelling on, on the, on the strategy side of things?

00:32:41.440 --> 00:32:46.400
I think mechanically there's, yeah, mechanically there's something here.

00:32:46.940 --> 00:32:54.360
Um, the, the differences are really just like, oh, this is Western made versus, you know, Eastern black.

00:32:54.660 --> 00:32:59.900
It's just a cold war thing, even though, you know, the Berlin wall, the Berlin wall fell a year ago.

00:32:59.900 --> 00:33:10.060
It's okay. Um, and I, I mean, yeah, if, if we're going only going by the mechanics and like the stats for each unit, like, yeah, this seems pretty balanced.

00:33:10.060 --> 00:33:17.240
They just have kind of different names and you look at the manual, the manual is basically like an actual like service manual.

00:33:17.840 --> 00:33:22.720
It's not like a fun, uh, with lots of color and illustrations.

00:33:22.720 --> 00:33:26.480
Like it's literally just, this is the stat of this plane.

00:33:26.660 --> 00:33:28.040
This is the stat of this tank.

00:33:28.200 --> 00:33:36.100
So you can just see there, like there's probably been a lot of work done in making this game, uh, mechanically balanced.

00:33:36.520 --> 00:33:46.080
And I can't confirm that because I'm not going to read the whole thing while I'm playing, even though I guess maybe I should have, but like, it seems to be.

00:33:46.080 --> 00:33:48.600
Yeah. I mean, and you give people enough time, right?

00:33:48.940 --> 00:33:51.020
Yeah. 2023 versus 1990.

00:33:51.400 --> 00:33:57.020
Like I'm sure that there are strategy guides somewhere on the internet that tell you how to cheese every single scenario, right?

00:33:57.120 --> 00:34:00.100
Like there's this weak point, take it out immediately.

